<title>USA: TENNIS-HINGIS TAKES OVER TOP SPOT IN WTA RANKINGS.</title>
<headline>TENNIS-HINGIS TAKES OVER TOP SPOT IN WTA RANKINGS.</headline>
<dateline>STAMFORD, Connecticut 1997-03-31</dateline>
<text>
<p>Sixteen-year-old Martina Hingis of Switzerland became the</p>
<p>youngest player ever to hold the world's top ranking Monday as</p>
<p>she passed Steffi Graf of Germany in the WTA Tour rankings:</p>
<p>  1. Martina Hingis (Switzerland)		4711.5000 points</p>
<p>  2. Steffi Graf (Germany)			 4249.5000</p>
<p>  3. Jana Novotna (Czech Republic)	     3427.4412</p>
<p>  4. Arantxa Sanchez Vicario (Spain)	   3409.6871</p>
<p>  5. Monica Seles (U.S.)			   3231.8083</p>
<p>  6. Conchita Martinez (Spain)		   2880.6710</p>
<p>  7. Anke Huber (Germany)			  2538.0388</p>
<p>  8. Lindsay Davenport (U.S.)		    2485.0667</p>
<p>  9. Irina Spirlea (Romania)		     2266.7950</p>
<p> 10. Iva Majoli (Croatia)			  2254.2820</p>
<p> 11. Mary Joe Fernandez (U.S.)		   1792.5113</p>
<p> 12. Karina Habsudova (Slovakia)		 1784.2182</p>
<p> 13. Barbara Paulus (Austria)		    1696.2813</p>
<p> 14. Mary Pierce (France)			  1594.8195</p>
<p> 15. Amanda Coetzer (South Africa)	     1489.7009</p>
<p> 16. Kimberly Po (U.S.)			    1462.0647</p>
<p> 17. Judith Wiesner (Austria)		    1428.5810</p>
<p> 18. Brenda Schultz-McCarthy (Netherlands)   1381.8799</p>
<p> 19. Elena Likhovtseva (Russia)		  1249.2240</p>
<p> 20. Sabine Appelmans (Belgium)		  1146.6050</p>
</text>
